---
import { FRAMEWORK_NAMES, FRAMEWORKS, PRODUCT_NAME } from '../../consts/product';
import { LINKS } from '../../consts/site';
import { SNIPPETS } from '../../consts/snippets';
import { listOf } from '../../lib/format';
import Actions from '../Actions.astro';
import Button from '../Button.astro';
import CodeCard from '../CodeCard.astro';
import Grid from '../Grid.astro';
import RuleList from '../RuleList.astro';
import Section from '../Section.astro';
import Stack from '../Stack.astro';

const { laravel, symfony } = FRAMEWORKS;
---

<Section id="frameworks" heading={`Ready for ${listOf(FRAMEWORK_NAMES)}`}>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Dedicated integration packages wire everything into your framework: tasks run on its queue, and the sweep that recovers
    lost work runs on its scheduler. They build on {PRODUCT_NAME}, so your project needs a license for it, and you install them
    with Composer once it has access.
  </Fragment>
  <Grid columns={2}>
    <Stack>
      <h3 class="t-title">{laravel.name}</h3>
      <CodeCard title="Terminal" snippet={SNIPPETS.laravelInstall} />
      <RuleList>
        <li>
          <strong>Installs itself.</strong> The service provider is discovered automatically and there is nothing to migrate.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Runs on your {laravel.name} queue.</strong> Every task and every delivery is a queued job, on the connection and
          queue you choose. Give a single tool a queue of its own, for example a dedicated <code class="ic">research</code> queue
          for long sub-agents.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Uses the stores you already have.</strong> Background work is kept in the workflow persistence your agents already
          use, taken from the container.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sweeps on its own.</strong> The sweep is added to your schedule, every minute, on one server, without overlapping.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tells you when it needs help.</strong> Set an email address and you're notified when a sweep fails, or takes longer
          than its minute because you've outgrown a single sweeper.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Built for your tests.</strong> Swap in <code class="ic">FakeRunner</code>, or use
          <code class="ic">Queue::fake()</code> as with any other job.
        </li>
      </RuleList>
      <CodeCard title="PHP" snippet={SNIPPETS.laravelServe} />
    </Stack>
    <Stack>
      <h3 class="t-title">{symfony.name}</h3>
      <CodeCard title="Terminal" snippet={SNIPPETS.symfonyInstall} />
      <RuleList>
        <li>
          <strong>A bundle that registers everything</strong> in the service container, so your agents receive the
          <code class="ic">Background</code> by autowiring.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Runs on your application's own queue and scheduler</strong>, in the processes {symfony.name} already runs, instead
          of a separate runner and crontab.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Uses the stores your agents already share</strong>, so there's nothing new to set up in your database.
        </li>
      </RuleList>
    </Stack>
  </Grid>
  <Actions>
    <Button href={LINKS.contact}>Talk to us</Button>
  </Actions>
</Section>
